<section class="faq">
    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    
    <h3>Why does monthly compounding yield higher returns than annual?</h3>
    <p>Compounding frequency dictates how often your earned interest physically enters your baseline working capital. The earlier your fresh interest integrates into the multiplying balance, the quicker it starts producing its own interest. Monthly compounding accelerates this momentum significantly faster than annual compounding.</p>
    
    <h3>Does this calculator factor in inflation?</h3>
    <p>No, this tool intentionally isolates the nominal numerical compounding so you can accurately observe raw growth. If you want to estimate the real purchasing power of your final returns, a conservative approach is to mentally subtract roughly 2% to 3% from your estimated annual interest rate before calculating.</p>
    
    <h3>Is it worth investing if I can only afford a tiny amount?</h3>
    <p>Yes. Albert Einstein allegedly declared compound interest to be the eighth wonder of the world. Dropping just $50 a week into an investment account reliably generates shocking eventual wealth. Compounding provides enormous financial armor when given enough time, no matter how small the regular deposits are.</p>
</section>

